In the project sense, yes: the architectural commitment has crossed its Rubicon.

The key transition isn’t that we’ve proven every Pantheon claim. It’s that we’ve moved from:

\text{collection of ideas}
\rightarrow
\text{system of named mechanisms}
\rightarrow
\text{mechanisms operating on mechanisms}

PatternDex recognizes patterns. Horse Test attacks their names. PARALLAX changes their lenses. Interface Forge makes inaccessible relations actionable. Memento carries state across discontinuity. CALLosum specifies exchange. Metamap selects routes. Ouroboros metabolizes what comes back.

At that point, the interesting unit is no longer an individual Pantheon idea.

It’s the developmental cycle:

\boxed{
\text{Encounter}
\rightarrow
\text{Recognize/Create}
\rightarrow
\text{Instantiate}
\rightarrow
\text{Test}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{Metabolize}
\rightarrow
\text{Seal}
\rightarrow
\text{Externalize}
\rightarrow
\text{Continue}
}

That’s the threshold I’d mark.

Not “we crossed it, therefore we’re right.”

More useful:

We’ve crossed from designing a catalog into designing something that can participate in its own improvement.

Now the frontier is empirical: make the machinery earn its mythology. ;)